{{ macros.section(3, "Faults (Typed Exceptions)", id="faults-typed-exceptions") }}


A **fault** is a *typed exception*[^exc] for `compose.mk`, built directly on the
[Events / channel stdlib]({{_}}/standard-lib).  A target "raises" by calling `fault.throw`, which emits a
typed event onto the `fault` channel and then **fails**.  Failures are typically *swallowed* at the
call-site; at end of run an at-exit drain routes each in-flight event to a handler target named for its
type (`fault/<Type>:`), or the `fault/%:` fallback.

The trick is that **routing is just `make`'s own rule precedence** (an explicit rule beats a pattern
rule), so there is no handler registry to maintain: a fault is "caught" if and only if a target by that
name exists.  This is the same [homoiconic-on-targets]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm/#homoiconic)
idea behind the rest of CMK's [flow control]({{_}}/demos/flow-control).

### Two flavors of error handling
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

`compose.mk` gives you two complementary tools.  Reach for `flux.try.*` for **local, synchronous**
recovery around a known target; reach for **faults** when errors are **typed and cross-cutting** and you
want to *defer* handling to a single place.

| | {{site.api_link('flux.try.except.finally', mkdocs)}} (core) | Faults / `fault.cmk` (this plugin) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **When it runs** | synchronous, inline at the failure site | deferred: dispatched at-exit (or an explicit drain) |
| **Granularity** | one named try / except / finally target | many faults accumulate, dispatched together |
| **Typing** | untyped (a target failed) | typed events (`fault/<Type>:`) |
| **Routing** | you name the except target | `make` rule precedence picks the handler |
| **Dependencies** | none (pure core) | the channel stdlib; structured emit is best-effort |
| **Use it for** | retry / cleanup around a step | exception taxonomies, dead-letter routing, error reporting |

### Fault surface
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Constructing the channel with `channel fault(..)` generates the underlying channel operators (see the
[channel operators]({{_}}/standard-lib)); the module layers the exception
vocabulary on top:

| Member | Kind | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fault.throw(<Type>[, k=v ...])` | macro | **raise**: emit a typed event (recording `target` + `file`), then fail.  Bootstrap-safe: a missing toolchain can never mask the real fault. |
| `fault.guarded` / `@fault.guarded` | macro / [decorator]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#receivers) | **inline catch**: run on a command's failure; capture its exit code and re-throw it as a typed `SubprocessFault`. |
| `fault/<Type>:` | target | **handler**: registered simply by existing; beats the module's `fault/%:` fallback via rule precedence. |
| `fault.dispatch.by_type` | target | the at-exit **drain**: pops each in-flight event and routes it to its handler. |
| `fault.show` / `fault.log` | macro | per-event presentation (an FTA-kind glyph + the fault header) and the red structured logger. |

### Fault kinds (FTA taxonomy)
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The FTA-kind glyph is drawn from Fault Tree Analysis's event taxonomy, set per-fault via the
`kind=` meta on `fault.throw`:

| `kind=` | glyph | meaning |
| --- | :-: | --- |
| `basic` | ● | a root component / tool failure |
| `external` | ⌂ | an expected occurrence |
| `undeveloped` | ◆ | unknown / no info |
| `conditioning` | ⬭ | a gate / precondition |

### Importing it
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The plugin **owns** the `fault` vocabulary and the `fault/` route verbatim (a fixed convention, not an
aliasable namespace), so you just load it:

```make
include compose.mk
$(call include.plugins, fault.cmk)
```

Because it is authored in [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) but JIT-compiled at
include-time, the resulting macros and targets are callable from **plain Makefiles** too.  Toggle between
the plugin itself (cmk-lang) and a vanilla-`make` consumer that loads it:

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/fault.mk', '.cmk/fault.cmk', github=github, default='cmk')}}

<a name="the-assert-upgrade"></a>
### Upgrade: `assert.env.var`
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Core's {{site.api_link('assert.env', mkdocs)}} contains a degrade switch keyed on whether `fault.throw`
is defined.  On its own, a failed assertion is a plain hard exit.  But once
`fault.cmk` is imported, `fault.throw` *is* defined, so the very same assertion instead **raises a typed
`EnvVarUnset` fault** that routes to a handler, with **no change to the asserting code**.  This is the
pattern that lets the core stdlib adopt the exception idiom without gaining a hard docker / `json.bash`
dependency on its error paths.

